"""just settings: each setting's value and where it was set, for one role, one variable or all of them.

    "$bin/python" tools/settings --usage [--json=true]
    "$bin/python" tools/settings [--vault-password-file <file>] [--all=true] [--changed=true] [--json=true] -- [NAME]

It asks Ansible itself, in-process, as a run would: the loader with the vault opened as Ansible's own commands open
it, the inventory, the variables of a play that names the role - on a host of the group that role's playbook runs it
on - and the templar for each value. Nothing of Ansible's precedence is written again here. What a setting is - each
role's argument_specs, the wiring, the sections of local.yml.example - comes from tools/docs.py, the same model the
wiki's reference pages are generated from (docs/architecture/custom-code.md §7.9).

It changes nothing and prints no secret:

- Lookups are held to an allowlist where Ansible loads every lookup, however one is reached: pipe runs a command,
  password writes a file. The inventory is read by the yaml plugin alone, never script, which runs what it finds.
- A value is masked when the vault file holds it (lock 1), when Ansible marks it as decrypted from a vault, an inline
  !vault included (lock 2), when its role marks the variable no_log (lock 3), or when its expression reads a name
  the vault holds or a no_log variable (lock 4). Before anything is printed, every value about to be printed is
  checked against every secret Ansible decrypted; a match prints nothing and exits 1 (lock 5).
- The vault's password file is the one the recipe passes (.vault_pass, where there is one), else what
  ansible-playbook would take; a vault that cannot be opened is refused before anything is printed.

Exits 0 with the report, 1 when it refuses.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

